<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Why do my hearing aids whistle only when I hug people or wear a mask?</h3>
<p>Hugs, hands, masks, hats, and even hair can reflect amplified sound right back into the microphones, briefly creating a feedback loop. Masks and glasses can also nudge the device or change how the dome seals. A tighter seal, different dome style, or small fit adjustment usually solves it.</p>

<h3>Can feedback damage my hearing or the aids?</h3>
<p>The squeal is annoying but not harmful at typical levels. The bigger issue is that it limits how much clean gain you can use, which can make speech harder to understand. Fixing the cause lets you listen at the right volume comfortably.</p>

<h3>Will switching to custom earmolds eliminate feedback?</h3>
<p>Custom molds often reduce feedback by creating a stable, deeper seal, especially for higher prescriptions. They don’t guarantee zero whistle—vent size, insertion depth, wax, and reflections still matter—but they raise the ceiling so you can use more gain without squeal.</p>

<h3>My aids used to be quiet. Why the sudden squeal?</h3>
<p>Common culprits include a blocked wax guard, cracked tubing, moisture, a loose dome, earwax buildup, or a change in ear shape/weight. Have your ears and devices checked, clean or replace the coupling parts, and re-run feedback calibration.</p>


